---
// /for-orgs/demo/ - Central Park Throughikers, a whole published org to walk
// through before typing anything (features/ORG_ONBOARDING.md, #1539).
//
// THE ONE STRUCTURAL RULE, and it is the reason this page mounts widgets
// rather than drawing its own tables: **the demo org mounts the real
// components.** The design says it outright - "the moment the demo gets its
// own copies it starts lying about the product." So the sections below are
// the same three public embeds an organization pastes onto their own site,
// pointed at `central-park-throughikers`. What a visitor sees here is what an
// org's own visitors see, because it is the same code answering.
//
// THE ORG IS INVENTED AND THE PAGE SAYS SO FIRST, above everything else.
// Central Park's coordinates are real and surveyed; the Throughikers, their
// people, their hours and their reports are made up. The real park is
// maintained by the Central Park Conservancy, who have nothing to do with
// this page - said here because a page that renders like a real org's page
// and is not one has to be labelled, not merely qualified in a footer.
//
// Every number on this page is @unvalidated and most are invented: six
// routes, 13.8 miles, 29 volunteers. They exist to make the layout dense
// enough to judge. The two real things are the coordinates and the shape of
// the widgets.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import OrgNav from '../../components/OrgNav.astro'
import { DEMO_API_BASE } from '../../lib/demoApi'

const title = 'OurHike — Central Park Throughikers, a demo org'
const description =
  'A whole published organization to walk through before you type anything: six routes, an invented roster, and the same widgets your own org would get.'

const ORG_SLUG = 'central-park-throughikers'
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <OrgNav />
  <div class="org-demo-banner" role="note">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p>
        <strong>This org is invented.</strong> The coordinates are real — the park's
        corners and its landmarks are where they actually are — but the
        Throughikers, their people, their hours and their reports are all made
        up so you can look around without signing anything. The real park is
        maintained by the Central Park Conservancy, who have nothing to do with
        this page.
      </p>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost btn--small" href="/for-orgs/">Leave the demo</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="org-eyebrow">NEW YORK, NY · 6 ROUTES · 13.8 MILES · 29 VOLUNTEERS</span>
      <h1 class="org-form__title">Central Park Throughikers</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Published since March 2026 · five of six routes have a maintainer · what
        a hiker sees is below, exactly as your own org's page would look.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>The view that matters most</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">
        <strong>This is what a volunteer actually opens.</strong> Every admin
        screen in this tool exists to produce this one. Sam is a Throughiker who
        looks after the Ramble — she never sees a registry or a pull request,
        only her own paths, what hikers said about them, and the three things she
        can do today.
      </p>
      <ul class="checks" style="margin-top: 16px">
        <li><strong>No second login.</strong> She signed into the Throughikers' own site and arrived here.</li>
        <li><strong>No jargon.</strong> Her section has a name, not an OBJECTID.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Works with no signal.</strong> Reports queue and go when she gets
          back — on her laptop or her phone.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top: 18px">
        <a class="btn" href={`/app/my/tread?org=${ORG_SLUG}`}
          >Open the full volunteer view</a
        >
      </p>
      <p class="org-help">
        Lives at <code class="mono">/my/tread</code> — its own address, so the welcome
        email and the org's own members area can both link straight to it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Their sections</h2>
      </div>
      {
        /* WHAT THIS ACTUALLY IS, corrected 2026-09-17. This lede used to
           promise "the same table and map your own admins sign off on", and
           the widget below it is the Hike Finder: an organization's hikes,
           with a name, a park and a length. The table an admin signs off on
           is the console's registry table - three tiers, blaze mapping, a
           status column - and it is not a public embed at all.

           A demo that describes one widget and shows another fails at the one
           job it has, which is being what a visitor to an organization's own
           site would see. */
      }
      <p class="lede">
        Their hikes, filterable, exactly as a visitor to their own site would
        get them — one widget, one behaviour. Narrow by region or length; the
        lengths are theirs, drawn from the sections they published.
      </p>
      {
        /* THE REAL WIDGET, not a copy of it. Same script tag an organization
           pastes onto their own site, same `data-org`. If this page drew its
           own table it would drift from the product the first time either
           changed, which is exactly what the design warns about. */
      }
      <div
        id="ourhike-hikes"
        data-org={ORG_SLUG}
        data-filters="region,difficulty,length"
        data-rows="25"
        style="margin-top: 20px"
      >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Their workdays</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">
        Upcoming work, as their own visitors see it. A workday mirrored from an
        org's own calendar sends its signup to <em>their</em> form and says so —
        an org with a working calendar is not asked to abandon it.
      </p>
      <div id="ourhike-workdays" data-org={ORG_SLUG} data-window="60" style="margin-top: 20px"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="blaze"></span>
        <h2>Their coverage badge</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lede">
        One snippet, two shapes — a strip for a footer and a card for a donate
        page. Every figure comes from what they published and confirmed: miles
        from their registry, volunteers from their roster, and hours from the
        ones a supervisor signed off.
      </p>
      <p class="org-help">
        <strong>Three counts, and never a list.</strong> It does not say which
        sections nobody is holding — a hiker reading that would make a routing
        decision out of an organization's staffing problem. The coverage report
        that does say is inside the console, for the people who can act on it.
      </p>
      <div id="ourhike-coverage" data-org={ORG_SLUG} style="margin-top: 20px"></div>
      <div
        id="ourhike-coverage-card"
        data-org={ORG_SLUG}
        data-shape="card"
        data-title="Our park, live"
        style="margin-top: 20px; max-width: 320px"
      >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <div class="org-note">
        <p>
          <strong>Everything above is one paste each.</strong> A div and a script
          tag, on the org's own page, reading the registry they already signed
          off. No cookies, no tracking, no OurHike branding they cannot remove,
          and it inherits their fonts and colours. If our servers are down their
          page still renders — the embed just does not appear.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top: 20px">
        <a class="btn" href="/for-orgs/#register">Add your own trails</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {
    /* WHERE THE EMBEDS READ FROM, set before the embed script loads.
       `ourhike.js` takes `window.__OURHIKE_API__` and, with nothing set,
       draws nothing - "an unconfigured page renders empty rather than
       pointing at a guessed host", which is right everywhere except the one
       page whose job is to show a whole published organization. So this
       points at four generated endpoints under this page, at exactly the
       paths the real API serves: see src/lib/demoApi.ts. The embed itself is
       unchanged, so what a visitor sees here is what an org's own visitors
       would see. */
  }
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ base: DEMO_API_BASE }}>
    window.__OURHIKE_API__ = base
  </script>
  <script src="/embed/v1/ourhike.js" defer is:inline></script>
</Base>
